{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1525300-bows-in-vda20","id":1525300,"title":"Bows in VDA20","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-11-10T02:34:59-08:00","id":1525300,"title":"Bows in VDA20","content":"Hello everyone!<br /> <br /> I was looking over the rules for bows in VDA20 and... is it just me or are they really not that useful for vampires?<br /> <br /> They do 2 lethal damage (4 with longbows, but they seem to mean the British longbow type specifically, rather than the common longbows available since prehistory) with no option to customise the bow to the archer's strength. Most of the damage would therefore be expected to come in game from the archer's extra successes on the Dexterity + Archery roll, with the advantage that occasionally one may shoot the first arrow ambushing, negating dodge, and that dodge maneuvers are difficulty 8 unless one activates Celerity. One may also use multiple actions (with rising difficulties to hit) to shoot one arrow per Archery dot per turn, but usually that means difficulties 7, 8 and 9 (and no attack can be made at difficulty 10 with multiple actions). A mastercraft bow (Tome of Secrets) makes that 6, 7, 8 and 9.<br /> <br /> In short: it just does not seem to put out enough damage to even cause one level of damage on many rolls, factoring dodge and soaking in. Of course, there is a distance advantage, but that's about it. I get the impression that most targets worth shooting wouldn't even be bothered by the arrows. Staking the heart is definitely not an option, with difficulty 9 to hit and a requirement of 3+ damage inflicted.<br /> Let's assume I have Dexterity 5, Archery 5 and Celerity 5 and am specialised in this roll. 15 dice at diff.9 with specialisation averages 3 successes. Let's assume the victim has been ambushed and does not dodge. I roll 5 dice for damage (2 of the bow + 3 of the successes, and I am using the rule that you roll all successes to hit rather than only those beyond the first) at 40% chances of success: average is 2 successes. The impaling has already failed and the victim only needs 5 dice to soak to suffer no damage at all on average. I I have Dexterity, Archery and Celerity all at 5, it's fair to imagine that a worthy target could have Stamina, Fortitude and Armor at 5, too, but would still be perfectly safe with much less.<br /> <br /> Unless one has Quietus Cruscitus to envenom the arrows or Celerity 7 Flower of Death to dramatically increase successes to hit, archery seems largely useless to vampires, except for circumstances where other things would do a better job anyway.<br /> <br /> Am I missing something?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-10T07:36:41-08:00","id":1525307,"title":"","content":"Well, define &quot;useful for vampires&quot;<br /> For what?<br /> <br /> So we are speaking of the Dark Ages - possibly antiquity<br /> When in came to wars and battles it was rare they fought during night - it happened but was rare<br /> Sure night battles are great for ambushes, and if you\u2019re about to win a battle you aren\u2019t going to just stop because it\u2019s getting dark., but it was rare.<br /> Attacking at dusk was also a thing that happened on occasion since it\u2019s one of the two times of day where the sun is low, giving one side an advantage<br /> Good luck commanding a large force with any semblance of control in the dark. Signaling becomes a literal nightmare. Identifying friend and foe? During night it becomes much more difficult if not outright impossible. Men are much more prone to panic and are probably tired, going to trip over or get lost.<br /> These difficulties it presented meant commanders often had minimal control over such battles.<br /> But of course sieges which lasted for weeks, months or even years the battles were often fought day... and night. But those were the sieges, a normal battle is different<br /> <br /> Lets move from the battles!<br /> <br /> Most if not all the ranged weapons are... near useless during night if you don't have some supernatural eyesight to compensate the night (Protean 1 for example, Auspex maybe)<br /> Vampires can't really see in the dark <i>that better</i> than the humans<br /> The human eye is evolved for being prey, not a predator at night.<br /> Sure the Vampires are &quot;nocturnal predators&quot;, but in reality, thats self-delusion\u200b, they are parasites but more importantly they are <i>cursed Humans</i> and their vision isn't more acute than a living human's vision (especially funny to remind a settite players, you have to stay in the dark but nothing provide you better eyesight. It is a curse <i>for a reason</i>)<br /> And those torches and fire bowls? Irritating the Beast stirs and you better stay away from them!<br /> <br /> So the main question is... <i>how </i>and <i>when</i> do you want to use your bow with a vampire and for <i>what</i>?<br /> Realistically probably never and for nothing<br /> Only maybe for nightly games in a courtyard, sport and &quot;fun&quot;<br /> <br /> You are right in the essence, bow are not that usefull for vampires, but not because the rules, but because they are operate at night.<br /> <br /> Precious few vampire you would find running on the streets chasing god knows (man hunt?) what shooting arrows at their victim (well could happen, but I guess that would be for fun or sport), a forest (unless it is a Tzimisce or Gangrel organized sport, &quot;game&quot;) or at the battlefield<br /> The moon and starlight is not the best for archery, it is not totally useless during night but very limited<br /> It could be fun to &quot;snipe&quot; a few attackers from attop the wall during the siege at night but.. basically thats the only time a vampire would need to touch a bow and could use it effectively i ncombat<br /> <br /> Sure, you could build a vampire with stats that potentially could use a bow.. .but it is simply not worth it<br /> But as I said, I don't think because the rules<br /> Because archery at night not that great idea<br /> \u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":23890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","user":"Shadeprowler","date":". 11-10-2023, 12:04 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23890-shadeprowler","name":"Shadeprowler","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23890_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-10T08:03:57-08:00","id":1525308,"title":"","content":"Bows vs vampires really comes down to the idea of volley firing a bunch of arrows without metal heads (to count as stakes) and hope that one out of the few dozen shots raining down will be a heart shot. But this requires a large group of archers, enough open terrain to pull this off, and the vampire to stick around once the first volley has landed.<br /> <br /> More realistically if you are doing an ambush scenario a crossbow firing a bolt would be the better option since you can constantly aim and wait for the optimal heart shot, whereas with a bow, especially the heavier ones, holding a drawn bow for any length of time is quite tiring.<br /> <br /> Using fire arrows to remotely light a prepared trap of oil and hay bales to cause the vampire to freak out or become trapped in a building is the safer option rather than trying to use the bow and arrow to directly do damage.<br /> <br /> As for rules for upgrading bows to deal with higher strength values, take a look at the smallest versions of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballista\" target=\"_blank\">Ballistae </a>which would simulate what a potence invested vampire could manually wield. While a mortal can't see much at night to aim, a Vampire who has Potence and either Auspex/Protean is essentially a very mobile siege weapon. A scorpion ballista would be about right for this sort of thing.<br /> <br /> In one V: DA game, a Tremere had an NPC coterie of Gargoyles under his command. Properly kitted out they could decimate entire fortifications and slaughter small armies. They naturally have potence which means they could use such upgraded bows/crossbows, Flight allowed them to be mobile and attack from multiple vectors, just add some flaming oil flasks to cause some chaos on the ground. Whoever survived the nightly raids eventually would be hunted down and taken out by simply landing on them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-10T12:11:08-08:00","id":1525322,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Shadeprowler</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1525300-bows-in-vda20?p=1525307#post1525307\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Well, define &quot;useful for vampires&quot;<br /> For what?<br /> <br /> So we are speaking of the Dark Ages - possibly antiquity<br /> When in came to wars and battles it was rare they fought during night - it happened but was rare<br /> Sure night battles are great for ambushes, and if you\u2019re about to win a battle you aren\u2019t going to just stop because it\u2019s getting dark., but it was rare.<br /> Attacking at dusk was also a thing that happened on occasion since it\u2019s one of the two times of day where the sun is low, giving one side an advantage<br /> Good luck commanding a large force with any semblance of control in the dark. Signaling becomes a literal nightmare. Identifying friend and foe? During night it becomes much more difficult if not outright impossible. Men are much more prone to panic and are probably tired, going to trip over or get lost.<br /> These difficulties it presented meant commanders often had minimal control over such battles.<br /> But of course sieges which lasted for weeks, months or even years the battles were often fought day... and night. But those were the sieges, a normal battle is different<br /> <br /> Lets move from the battles!<br /> <br /> Most if not all the ranged weapons are... near useless during night if you don't have some supernatural eyesight to compensate the night (Protean 1 for example, Auspex maybe)<br /> Vampires can't really see in the dark <i>that better</i> than the humans<br /> The human eye is evolved for being prey, not a predator at night.<br /> Sure the Vampires are &quot;nocturnal predators&quot;, but in reality, thats self-delusion\u200b, they are parasites but more importantly they are <i>cursed Humans</i> and their vision isn't more acute than a living human's vision (especially funny to remind a settite players, you have to stay in the dark but nothing provide you better eyesight. It is a curse <i>for a reason</i>)<br /> And those torches and fire bowls? Irritating the Beast stirs and you better stay away from them!<br /> <br /> So the main question is... <i>how </i>and <i>when</i> do you want to use your bow with a vampire and for <i>what</i>?<br /> Realistically probably never and for nothing<br /> Only maybe for nightly games in a courtyard, sport and &quot;fun&quot;<br /> <br /> You are right in the essence, bow are not that usefull for vampires, but not because the rules, but because they are operate at night.<br /> <br /> Precious few vampire you would find running on the streets chasing god knows (man hunt?) what shooting arrows at their victim (well could happen, but I guess that would be for fun or sport), a forest (unless it is a Tzimisce or Gangrel organized sport, &quot;game&quot;) or at the battlefield<br /> The moon and starlight is not the best for archery, it is not totally useless during night but very limited<br /> It could be fun to &quot;snipe&quot; a few attackers from attop the wall during the siege at night but.. basically thats the only time a vampire would need to touch a bow and could use it effectively i ncombat<br /> <br /> Sure, you could build a vampire with stats that potentially could use a bow.. .but it is simply not worth it<br /> But as I said, I don't think because the rules<br /> Because archery at night not that great idea<br /> \u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, we seem to agree. I didn't mention battles or hunting because they were already obviously not relevant to most vampires most of the time. So I focused on combat or assassination or similar things, where it's still ultimately pointless to spend dots in Archery.<br /> <br /> As for Setites, depending on the edition, the serpent tongue does remove or mitigate darkness penalties. Unfortunately it does not explain why or how. If one likes snakes and animals in general, the reason is clear: snakes smell through their tongue and some also have heat-sensing (though I'm not sure that's tongue-related), and their tongue-empowered smell is so powerful and accurate that they can accurately bite from a distance in the dark. So a detail-loving ST might describe through smell the info the vampire gets in the dark, but including precise location, which is something we as humans are not used to get much through smell. It does, however, also open the door for discussions between players and STs over what the vampire is actually aware of and what not, as the text in VDA20, for example, only says that penalties in the dark do not apply if the tongue is out.<br /> Part of the darkness issue depends on how the ST describes environments and handles sources of light, too.<br /> <br /> Also, thrown weapons make sense both narratively for vampires with a fighting background - many types of soldier carried some kind of thrown weapon to soften enemy ranks before impact, and you can still throw weapons in a skirmish, let alone assassination attempts - and rules-wise - Strength + X damage is competitive with hand-to-hand weapons and can inflict damage and also be augmented by Potence or blood expenditure. The real issue is bows. They're ok between humans (who may be too poor to afford heavy armor and do not soam lethal damage), but I would have warrior clans and bloodlines quickly teach their childers that archery is not a great skill among Cainites.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-10T14:50:41-08:00","id":1525330,"title":"","content":"Snake tongues do not play any part in thermosensing; that's a property isolated to certain snakes that have noticeable pits in their face, including pit vipers and pythons, mostly, and these sense organs hook up to the same main nerves that go into the face and eyes. Colubrids like the so-called Egyptian &quot;asp&quot; (actually a form of true cobra) would not possess this capability.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-10T16:02:52-08:00","id":1525332,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1525300-bows-in-vda20?p=1525330#post1525330\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Snake tongues do not play any part in thermosensing; that's a property isolated to certain snakes that have noticeable pits in their face, including pit vipers and pythons, mostly, and these sense organs hook up to the same main nerves that go into the face and eyes. Colubrids like the so-called Egyptian &quot;asp&quot; (actually a form of true cobra) would not possess this capability.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, that was it! That's why I wasn't sure thermosensing had to do with the tongue. I kinda remembered it was some other organ. Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-10T16:05:47-08:00","id":1525333,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Thoth</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1525300-bows-in-vda20?p=1525308#post1525308\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Bows vs vampires really comes down to the idea of volley firing a bunch of arrows without metal heads (to count as stakes) and hope that one out of the few dozen shots raining down will be a heart shot. But this requires a large group of archers, enough open terrain to pull this off, and the vampire to stick around once the first volley has landed.<br /> <br /> More realistically if you are doing an ambush scenario a crossbow firing a bolt would be the better option since you can constantly aim and wait for the optimal heart shot, whereas with a bow, especially the heavier ones, holding a drawn bow for any length of time is quite tiring.<br /> <br /> Using fire arrows to remotely light a prepared trap of oil and hay bales to cause the vampire to freak out or become trapped in a building is the safer option rather than trying to use the bow and arrow to directly do damage.<br /> <br /> As for rules for upgrading bows to deal with higher strength values, take a look at the smallest versions of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ballista\" target=\"_blank\">Ballistae </a>which would simulate what a potence invested vampire could manually wield. While a mortal can't see much at night to aim, a Vampire who has Potence and either Auspex/Protean is essentially a very mobile siege weapon. A scorpion ballista would be about right for this sort of thing.<br /> <br /> In one V: DA game, a Tremere had an NPC coterie of Gargoyles under his command. Properly kitted out they could decimate entire fortifications and slaughter small armies. They naturally have potence which means they could use such upgraded bows/crossbows, Flight allowed them to be mobile and attack from multiple vectors, just add some flaming oil flasks to cause some chaos on the ground. Whoever survived the nightly raids eventually would be hunted down and taken out by simply landing on them.</div> </div> </div> </div>I like the idea of the scorpion ballista, although it would only work for characters that are not worrying about keeping the Masquerade (or its medieval analogue), like the gargoyle band you mentioned.<br /> <br /> Agreed on the rest.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-11T10:41:40-08:00","id":1525349,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1525300-bows-in-vda20?p=1525333#post1525333\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like the idea of the scorpion ballista, although it would only work for characters that are not worrying about keeping the Masquerade (or its medieval analogue), like the gargoyle band you mentioned.<br /> <br /> Agreed on the rest.<br /> \u200b\u200b\u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>If you want to preserve the masquerade, I think the best middle ground way of using archery with potence would be to use middle ages full metal crossbows, with the potence score allowing the vampire to manually reset the crossbow by hand rather than requiring a windlass or similar loading/priming mechanism. This would give them a faster rate of fire compared to mortals, but it wouldn't inherently be masquerade breaking or game breaking.<br /> <br /> The poor ST just has to take a crash course on medieval crossbow loading speeds and try to remember that those heavier crossbows could defeat a lot of armor. On the plus side, with that kind of potence score, the vampire could probably produce a lot of bolts very quickly assuming you have the base materials and they don't freak out in the smithy.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/727-thoth","name":"Thoth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar727_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1689"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-12T09:26:28-08:00","id":1525372,"title":"","content":"Yeah, the V20 Dark Ages weapons rules and tables were the reason I wrote a Storyteller's Vault supplement about weapons and armor...<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Freelance Writer and Storyteller's Vault contributor. Find my work here: <a href=\"http://www.storytellersvault.com/index.php?affiliate_id=17903\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.storytellersvault.com/ind...liate_id=17903</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1535-jackob","name":"Jackob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1535_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-12T09:48:43-08:00","id":1525374,"title":"","content":"Minimum Strength ratings are something I find particularly baffling. Perhaps I wasn't holding the swords in question for very long, but it doesn't seem like you need to be super jacked to wield most of a medieval armory... except, ironically, for the long bows.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-12T10:54:21-08:00","id":1525377,"title":"","content":"One-handing an axe or a flanged mace needs some strength, but by 1242, you won\u2019t need above-average strength to use an arming sword. A great-sword-of-war, on the other hand\u2026","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Freelance Writer and Storyteller's Vault contributor. Find my work here: <a href=\"http://www.storytellersvault.com/index.php?affiliate_id=17903\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.storytellersvault.com/ind...liate_id=17903</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1535-jackob","name":"Jackob","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1535_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"293"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-16T04:47:22-08:00","id":1525506,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hermeticus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1525300-bows-in-vda20#post1525300\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hello everyone!<br /> <br /> I was looking over the rules for bows in VDA20 and... is it just me or are they really not that useful for vampires?<br /> <br /> They do 2 lethal damage (4 with longbows, but they seem to mean the British longbow type specifically, rather than the common longbows available since prehistory) with no option to customise the bow to the archer's strength. Most of the damage would therefore be expected to come in game from the archer's extra successes on the Dexterity + Archery roll, with the advantage that occasionally one may shoot the first arrow ambushing, negating dodge, and that dodge maneuvers are difficulty 8 unless one activates Celerity. One may also use multiple actions (with rising difficulties to hit) to shoot one arrow per Archery dot per turn, but usually that means difficulties 7, 8 and 9 (and no attack can be made at difficulty 10 with multiple actions). A mastercraft bow (Tome of Secrets) makes that 6, 7, 8 and 9.<br /> <br /> In short: it just does not seem to put out enough damage to even cause one level of damage on many rolls, factoring dodge and soaking in. Of course, there is a distance advantage, but that's about it. I get the impression that most targets worth shooting wouldn't even be bothered by the arrows. Staking the heart is definitely not an option, with difficulty 9 to hit and a requirement of 3+ damage inflicted.<br /> Let's assume I have Dexterity 5, Archery 5 and Celerity 5 and am specialised in this roll. 15 dice at diff.9 with specialisation averages 3 successes. Let's assume the victim has been ambushed and does not dodge. I roll 5 dice for damage (2 of the bow + 3 of the successes, and I am using the rule that you roll all successes to hit rather than only those beyond the first) at 40% chances of success: average is 2 successes. The impaling has already failed and the victim only needs 5 dice to soak to suffer no damage at all on average. I I have Dexterity, Archery and Celerity all at 5, it's fair to imagine that a worthy target could have Stamina, Fortitude and Armor at 5, too, but would still be perfectly safe with much less.<br /> <br /> Unless one has Quietus Cruscitus to envenom the arrows or Celerity 7 Flower of Death to dramatically increase successes to hit, archery seems largely useless to vampires, except for circumstances where other things would do a better job anyway.<br /> <br /> Am I missing something?</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe the initial question is wrong. Why should arrows be useful against Vampires? The damage of an arrow in humans comes from disruption of bodily functions that doesn't happen in vampires. An arrow is just a small puncture, unless it goes through the heart. Similar to bullets.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26110-ramon-nogueras","name":"Ramon Nogueras","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-16T05:51:19-08:00","id":1525507,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramon Nogueras</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1525300-bows-in-vda20?p=1525506#post1525506\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Maybe the initial question is wrong. Why should arrows be useful against Vampires? The damage of an arrow in humans comes from disruption of bodily functions that doesn't happen in vampires. An arrow is just a small puncture, unless it goes through the heart. Similar to bullets.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a very good point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/16965-hermeticus","name":"Hermeticus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"431"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-16T07:36:42-08:00","id":1525510,"title":"","content":"I mean, I see how an axe or a sword can be a problem, you can be dismembered. But having small punctures all over your body, when blood loss and organ damage is irrelevant to you...<br /> <br /> Arrows may be useful. You can try and pin someone to a wall to slow him down, shoot the eyes to gain some advantage, even try to pierce the heart if the target is unarmored. But one of the perks of being a vampire, I think, is that you can disregard many sources of damage. So, if arrows are ineffective against vampires, so be it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26110-ramon-nogueras","name":"Ramon Nogueras","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-11-17T12:49:20-08:00","id":1525568,"title":"","content":"I think some of these arrowheads might do more than small punctures. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"b-bbcode b-bbcode__url b-bbcode__url--preview\"> <a href=\"https://www.longbow-archers.com/arrowheads.html\" target=\"_blank\" class=\"urlimg_container\"> <img class=\"js-img-fallback\" src=\"https://www.longbow-archers.com/images/logoLA.jpg\"> </a> <div class=\"urlinfo\"> <div class=\"title ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.longbow-archers.com/arrowheads.html\" target=\"_blank\"> Arrowheads </a> </div> <div class=\"url ellipsis\"> <a href=\"https://www.longbow-archers.com/arrowheads.html\" target=\"_blank\"> https://www.longbow-archers.com/arrowheads.html </a> </div> <div class=\"meta\"> Longbow Archers is a Reference Site for the Longbow with direct link in History, Associations, Competitions, Technical, latest Research and Trends </div> </div> </div><br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Freelance Writer and Storyteller's Vault contributor. Find my work here: <a href=\"http://www.storytellersvault.com/index.php?affiliate_id=17903\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.storytellersvault.com/ind...liate_id=17903</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1535-jackob","name":"Jackob","a